hello this is steve kaufman  uh i'm going to get back to this whole  subject of the korean uh golfers i mean  that's we talk about the lady  the  ladies professional golf association  which is based in the united states  that has come out recently and said that  their members  will have to be able to give interviews  in english and so forth uh it more or  less has been identified that the main  target is the korean golfers  because the koreans are so dominant now  there are mexican and taiwanese and i  don't know  what other foreign golfers um but they  either don't do very well or or in or in  the case of  those that do well they speak english  well  uh so there's been this tremendous  reaction all of the professional  anti-racist organizations are up in arms  it's an opportunity for them to stop  their stuff the  asian-american academics association and  all kinds of hand wringing is going on  and  i mean i just think it's it i'm just  amazed the um no one said that you have  to speak english in order to play golf  somewhere  this is not about discrimination the  lpga is a product  it's a it's a brand name  and the people who run that brand name  have the right to decide what  to expect of their players and if they  feel that it's important for their  players to be able to communicate  through acceptance speeches when they  win through uh interviews  uh interacting with key sponsors  i mean that's how  they can decide what the requirements of  of the job are i i just i don't  understand the issue  uh yeah there may be people who don't  like seeing so many koreans on the golf  on the ladies golf tour there may even  be people within the lpga who feel that  way  for all i know  there are maybe americans who don't like  koreans there's probably koreans who  don't like americans  that's not against the law  and the people at the lpga didn't say we  don't like koreans we don't want koreans  we don't want foreigners all they said  was we want our playboy players to be  able to communicate in english  uh they said effectively now  to me that's not difficult  uh if those foreign players are in the  united states  for months and months and months  and they have time and they're young and  they had english at school if they're  motivated they can learn enough to  conduct an interview  and they don't need a tutor  they don't need um  rosetta stone  what they need  is  an ipod either one of these  or the little truffle that i use  and it would not be difficult to create  a whole bunch of sample interviews or to  into take some interviews that other  english speakers give  provide transcript  and have these players listen to these  interviews for hours and hours  and then  if they're really smart  they would come to link  and then they would be able to use our  system to learn the language from this  material that's all they need to do in  six months they would all be able to  give interviews the worst thing would be  if the lpga hired some group of language  teachers  to come up with a test which is going to  test them for grammatical accuracy  nobody is interested in their in these  korean players grammatical accuracy  they are only the lpga wants them to be  able to communicate at some level now it  may be  that these korean players are under the  impression that they have to speak  perfect english  no  that and that would be very silly  if the lpga said they have to achieve  some level on some  test that would be very silly just to be  able to communicate so that the fans and  sponsors can actually  directly hear  what this player has to say in a  language that they can understand  i don't think that there is you know  abundant racism i don't think  tiger woods or  michelle we have heard professional golf  i know that many of these sports  organizations are interested in taking  their product  abroad into asia lots of people live in  asia japan korea china and have made  efforts in that direction i don't know  what the lpga has done  so  yeah there may be individual people who  don't like certain other people but the  issue here why not just keep it simple  what they've said is we want our players  to be able to communicate  in interviews in english  what's so difficult  uh  and i know  and i  take the opportunity to toot my own horn  but  link would work for them better than any  rosetta stone  and  there's abundant  interviews and conversations that  transcripts can be provided and all  these players have to do is listen for  six months and they would be quite  capable of providing an interview  and i mean in my own case we don't have  korean on link  uh but the principle is the same a lot  of listening reading and working on  vocabulary it's this it's what we make  more efficient at link but that's  basically how you learn languages don't  bother giving your interview for six  months don't bother even talking for six  months don't get a tutor don't waste  your time just get out your ipod and  listen a lot and get the language into  your brain  now i did that with korean about three  or four years ago i did it for about six  months  uh an hour a day here and there and uh  it's been three years so i've forgotten  a lot but i'm going to say a few words  in korean just to demonstrate that one  needing to be one needn't be afraid of  making mistakes and what i'm about to  say is going to be full of mistakes but  it doesn't matter because a korean  person will understand me  and may i mean  they don't care about me speaking korean  but if i were a pro golfer that had just  won  the  korean golf championship and i was  interviewed and was able to say  something in korean  the fans would appreciate it they would  make that would make me popular they  would want me back  so um now here goes my korean  uh  um  um  uh  anyway you get the picture i'm  struggling okay i haven't been at it for  a while  so but if i decided i mean  particularly in the case of giving  interviews i mean the fact is that those  interviews are pretty brain dead well  you know i had to keep you know  you know an even keel out there play one  hole at a time and focus on  whatever and  i don't know what all they say but  there's not many different variations in  the interview so to develop the ability  to do an interview it's not that  difficult and i think the other thing is  that they have to want to communicate i  mean it's their tournament as much as it  is or at least it's their tour as much  as it is anyone else's they're part of  that community they are all together  promoting that package so they  should want to be part of the community  talk to the other golfers  do things to make it better and i read  in an interview that  sari park and some of the other korean  golfers said yeah  they don't like the fact that there's  kind of they're going to be fined or  kicked out or something obviously but  um they agree with the principle and  apparently they haven't done enough to  get better despite pressure from the  lpga so now there's been this publicity  i think it's a good thing and i think  we're going to see them all  do a better job with their english and  that's what i have to say

